1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,654
Answering Slack messages in thirty seconds

2
00:00:02,654 --> 00:00:03,728
feels productive.

3
00:00:03,728 --> 00:00:06,381
Computer scientist Cal Newport argues this

4
00:00:06,381 --> 00:00:08,592
habit of constant motion crowds out

5
00:00:08,592 --> 00:00:09,540
your best work.

6
00:00:09,540 --> 00:00:11,292
In his book Slow Productivity,

7
00:00:11,292 --> 00:00:15,088
Newport diagnoses modern knowledge work with pseudo-productivity:

8
00:00:15,088 --> 00:00:16,431
using visible busyness,

9
00:00:16,431 --> 00:00:17,423
packed calendars,

10
00:00:17,423 --> 00:00:19,350
and instant replies as a stand-in

11
00:00:19,350 --> 00:00:20,460
for useful results.

12
00:00:20,460 --> 00:00:22,522
Every time you juggle seven active

13
00:00:22,522 --> 00:00:24,523
projects and thirty chat threads,

14
00:00:24,523 --> 00:00:27,434
context-switching taxes your brain until nothing

15
00:00:27,434 --> 00:00:29,860
meaningful ever crosses the finish line.

16
00:00:29,860 --> 00:00:31,966
Newport's cure has three rules:

17
00:00:31,966 --> 00:00:34,208
cap major active projects at two,

18
00:00:34,208 --> 00:00:36,654
work at a sustainable seasonal pace,

19
00:00:36,654 --> 00:00:39,440
and obsess over undeniable craft quality.

20
00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:40,809
Five years from now,

21
00:00:40,809 --> 00:00:43,272
nobody will remember how quickly you

22
00:00:43,272 --> 00:00:44,572
cleared your inbox,

23
00:00:44,572 --> 00:00:47,036
they will only remember the flagship

24
00:00:47,036 --> 00:00:49,020
assets you actually finished.
